WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:03.290
- [Narrator] The U.S. economy
lost more than 20.5 million

00:00:03.290 --> 00:00:07.340
jobs in April, and 1.4 million
of them were in healthcare,

00:00:07.340 --> 00:00:10.443
a sector that has historically
been recession-proof.

00:00:11.550 --> 00:00:15.940
This chart shows employment
numbers between 1990 and 2020.

00:00:15.940 --> 00:00:18.440
This downturn is the great
recession, where millions

00:00:18.440 --> 00:00:20.610
of workers were laid off.

00:00:20.610 --> 00:00:23.170
You can see healthcare
jobs continue to rise

00:00:23.170 --> 00:00:25.530
during that same period.

00:00:25.530 --> 00:00:28.940
And the same can be seen
during other recessions, too.

00:00:28.940 --> 00:00:31.550
But this time it's different,
and experts say that

00:00:31.550 --> 00:00:34.640
the impact from the coronavirus
crisis could fundamentally

00:00:34.640 --> 00:00:37.430
change the healthcare
sector, and a lot of the jobs

00:00:37.430 --> 00:00:40.260
lost are not likely to return.

00:00:40.260 --> 00:00:43.520
- Healthcare has been among
the most resilient industries

00:00:43.520 --> 00:00:46.520
in the economy, and there are
really two reasons for this.

00:00:46.520 --> 00:00:48.660
- [Narrator] David Cutler
is an economics professor

00:00:48.660 --> 00:00:51.170
at Harvard, and has done a
lot of work understanding

00:00:51.170 --> 00:00:54.100
how the medical system
operates, and how it responds

00:00:54.100 --> 00:00:56.490
to events like COVID-19.

00:00:56.490 --> 00:00:59.720
- One is because people have insurance,

00:00:59.720 --> 00:01:03.160
and second is because
people's illness tends not

00:01:03.160 --> 00:01:07.020
to vary too much over
expansions and recessions.

00:01:07.020 --> 00:01:10.550
Since the pandemic, anything
that's not emergency,

00:01:10.550 --> 00:01:15.070
people have cut enormously,
and even many emergencies,

00:01:15.070 --> 00:01:18.500
use of care for emergencies
is way, way down.

00:01:18.500 --> 00:01:20.180
- [Narrator] To help the
sector cope with the virus,

00:01:20.180 --> 00:01:22.610
some healthcare restrictions
were relaxed as part

00:01:22.610 --> 00:01:26.880
of the $8.3 billion
coronavirus stimulus package.

00:01:26.880 --> 00:01:28.990
That gave a big boost for different parts

00:01:28.990 --> 00:01:32.040
of the healthcare industry,
including telemedicine,

00:01:32.040 --> 00:01:36.340
that saw one company report a
50% rise in telemed services.

00:01:36.340 --> 00:01:39.340
- Now people both in private
insurance and in Medicare

00:01:39.340 --> 00:01:42.850
are using telemedicine
a lot, it's hoped that

00:01:42.850 --> 00:01:45.800
that can then become a
usual part of practice.

00:01:45.800 --> 00:01:48.830
On a scope of practice
front, we enabled a lot

00:01:48.830 --> 00:01:51.270
of nurse-practitioners,
medical assistants,

00:01:51.270 --> 00:01:55.000
physicians' assistants to do
more, now that we've done that,

00:01:55.000 --> 00:01:56.710
we want to be able to keep that.

00:01:56.710 --> 00:01:59.420
And the third one which
is really important is,

00:01:59.420 --> 00:02:02.760
most physicians' practices
and hospitals get

00:02:02.760 --> 00:02:04.870
their money by doing more services.

00:02:04.870 --> 00:02:06.700
When people don't go to the doctor,

00:02:06.700 --> 00:02:08.050
the providers lose money.

00:02:08.050 --> 00:02:11.430
A way out of that is to pay
on a fixed, say per-patient-

00:02:11.430 --> 00:02:14.360
per-month basis, and so
that's another change people

00:02:14.360 --> 00:02:17.690
would like to see become
a permanent change.

00:02:17.690 --> 00:02:19.570
- [Narrator] Another reason
a rebound in healthcare

00:02:19.570 --> 00:02:21.300
will be harder than in previous years

00:02:21.300 --> 00:02:24.370
is people's financial
exposure to healthcare.

00:02:24.370 --> 00:02:27.000
Just looking at the last
recession, and the out-of-pocket

00:02:27.000 --> 00:02:29.950
expenses were far less
than what they are today.

00:02:29.950 --> 00:02:34.950
- If you're in a big
recession, and you now learn

00:02:35.030 --> 00:02:37.690
that you're gonna pay $2500 out-of-pocket

00:02:37.690 --> 00:02:39.120
before insurance pays anything,

00:02:39.120 --> 00:02:41.720
people may decide they're
happier cutting back,

00:02:41.720 --> 00:02:44.530
particularly since they just
had experience cutting back

00:02:44.530 --> 00:02:47.800
during the first phase
of the COVID epidemic.

00:02:47.800 --> 00:02:51.610
If you then add on top of
that what the potential,

00:02:51.610 --> 00:02:54.490
for example, COVID to
come back in late summer

00:02:54.490 --> 00:02:57.490
or fall, and by that point,
you really have people

00:02:57.490 --> 00:03:00.050
with a very different
outlook for what they want

00:03:00.050 --> 00:03:03.140
in the medical system, providers
in very different shape,

00:03:03.140 --> 00:03:07.000
and people in a financial
situation where they may not want

00:03:07.000 --> 00:03:09.740
to go back to the care
they were receiving.

00:03:09.740 --> 00:03:11.660
- [Narrator] Healthcare in
the U.S. continues to be

00:03:11.660 --> 00:03:14.220
one of the most debated
issues among lawmakers

00:03:14.220 --> 00:03:17.490
and politicians and on a
global level, the U.S. spends

00:03:17.490 --> 00:03:21.440
at least 17% of GDP on
healthcare, whereas most other

00:03:21.440 --> 00:03:25.100
developed countries spend
50% less, on average.

00:03:25.100 --> 00:03:27.380
So while it is critical
for the U.S. economy

00:03:27.380 --> 00:03:29.640
that healthcare rebounds
and that it continues

00:03:29.640 --> 00:03:32.280
to provide care for those
who regularly need it,

00:03:32.280 --> 00:03:34.940
economists are watching
for signs that it emerges

00:03:34.940 --> 00:03:37.290
from the pandemic more streamlined.

00:03:37.290 --> 00:03:40.750
- So it's really a very bad
idea to have a healthcare

00:03:40.750 --> 00:03:43.210
system that's bloated, they pay a lot

00:03:43.210 --> 00:03:44.840
and they don't get what they need.

00:03:44.840 --> 00:03:48.520
Now the best way to deal
with this is not to just shut

00:03:48.520 --> 00:03:51.150
everything off at once and
lay off a ton of people,

00:03:51.150 --> 00:03:54.500
but to gradually do things
so that you make that change.

00:03:54.500 --> 00:03:58.040
So what I would hope is that
after we make it through

00:03:58.040 --> 00:04:01.200
the first part of the
epidemic phase, we come back

00:04:01.200 --> 00:04:04.520
in a way that's more
streamlined, so that healthcare

00:04:04.520 --> 00:04:07.440
isn't as expensive and
then we help those people

00:04:07.440 --> 00:04:10.530
who we're not bringing back to
find other really productive

00:04:10.530 --> 00:04:12.180
things that they can do, as well.

